ng Page Layout Necessity</span></button></div><div style=3D"font-size: 14px=
; color: var(--text-primary);"><p style=3D"margin: 0px 0px 8px; line-height=
: 1.65;">You are asking a very sharp database engineering question.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0px 0px 8px; line-height: 1.65;">You are completely rig=
ht: with your current design (<code class=3D"mono" style=3D"background: var=
(--bg-raised); border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle); border-radius: 4px; =
padding: 1px 5px; font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--text-primary);">o=
ffset =3D sizeof(Header) + index * sizeof(Record)</code>), there is zero ex=
ternal fragmentation. The math is perfectly predictable, and finding a reco=
rd is O(1) disk seek.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0px 0px 8px; line-height: 1.65;">So why do real databas=
es (like PostgreSQL, SQLite, or MySQL) use a <strong style=3D"font-weight: =
600; color: var(--text-primary);">Slotted Page</strong> architecture instea=
d of a flat array of structs?</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0px 0px 8px; line-height: 1.65;">Here are the 3 main re=
asons you will need to switch to pages as you build out <code class=3D"mono=
" style=3D"background: var(--bg-raised); border: 1px solid var(--border-sub=
tle); border-radius: 4px; padding: 1px 5px; font-size: var(--text-sm); colo=
r: var(--text-primary);">minidb</code>:</p>
<h3 style=3D"font-size: var(--text-md); font-weight: 600; margin: 12px 0px =
6px;">1. Variable-Length Data (The 64-byte waste)</h3>
<p style=3D"margin: 0px 0px 8px; line-height: 1.65;">Right now, your <code =
class=3D"mono" style=3D"background: var(--bg-raised); border: 1px solid var=
(--border-subtle); border-radius: 4px; padding: 1px 5px; font-size: var(--t=
ext-sm); color: var(--text-primary);">Record</code> struct hardcodes <code =
class=3D"mono" style=3D"background: var(--bg-raised); border: 1px solid var=
(--border-subtle); border-radius: 4px; padding: 1px 5px; font-size: var(--t=
ext-sm); color: var(--text-primary);">char values[16][64]</code>.
If a user inserts the name <code class=3D"mono" style=3D"background: var(--=
bg-raised); border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle); border-radius: 4px; pad=
ding: 1px 5px; font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--text-primary);">"aud=
i"</code>, it takes up 4 bytes, but your database writes 64 bytes to the di=
sk. The remaining 60 bytes are wasted space (this is called <em style=3D"fo=
nt-style: italic;">internal fragmentation</em>).
<strong style=3D"font-weight: 600; color: var(--text-primary);">How Slotted=
 Pages fix this:</strong> A slotted page stores an array of "slots" (pointe=
rs/offsets) at the front of the page, and the actual raw string data grows =
backwards from the end of the page. This allows you to store variable-lengt=
h strings packed tightly together, saving huge amounts of disk space.</p>
<h3 style=3D"font-size: var(--text-md); font-weight: 600; margin: 12px 0px =
6px;">2. The Buffer Pool Manager needs fixed blocks</h3>
<p style=3D"margin: 0px 0px 8px; line-height: 1.65;">Right now, every time =
you do <code class=3D"mono" style=3D"background: var(--bg-raised); border: =
1px solid var(--border-subtle); border-radius: 4px; padding: 1px 5px; font-=
size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--text-primary);">execute_select</code> or=
 <code class=3D"mono" style=3D"background: var(--bg-raised); border: 1px so=
lid var(--border-subtle); border-radius: 4px; padding: 1px 5px; font-size: =
var(--text-sm); color: var(--text-primary);">execute_insert</code>, you are=
 making an <code class=3D"mono" style=3D"background: var(--bg-raised); bord=
er: 1px solid var(--border-subtle); border-radius: 4px; padding: 1px 5px; f=
ont-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--text-primary);">fread</code> or <cod=
e class=3D"mono" style=3D"background: var(--bg-raised); border: 1px solid v=
ar(--border-subtle); border-radius: 4px; padding: 1px 5px; font-size: var(-=
-text-sm); color: var(--text-primary);">fwrite</code> system call. Disk I/O=
 is the slowest thing a computer can do.
Real databases cache data in RAM using a <strong style=3D"font-weight: 600;=
 color: var(--text-primary);">Buffer Pool Manager</strong>. The OS and disk=
s work in 4KB chunks. To cache data, you need to read a 4KB "Page" from the=
 file into memory, do your reads/writes in RAM, and only flush the 4KB bloc=
k back to disk when needed. You cannot effectively cache a flat file of str=
ucts without page boundaries.</p>
<h3 style=3D"font-size: var(--text-md); font-weight: 600; margin: 12px 0px =
6px;">3. B+ Trees are built out of Pages</h3>
<p style=3D"margin: 0px 0px 8px; line-height: 1.65;">You have an empty <cod=
e class=3D"mono" style=3D"background: var(--bg-raised); border: 1px solid v=
ar(--border-subtle); border-radius: 4px; padding: 1px 5px; font-size: var(-=
-text-sm); color: var(--text-primary);">btree/</code> directory waiting to =
be built. A B+ Tree does not store individual rows scattered around; a sing=
le node in a B+ Tree <strong style=3D"font-weight: 600; color: var(--text-p=
rimary);">is</strong> a Page.
When the tree traverses down to find a record, it loads a 4KB Page, does bi=
nary search on the slots inside that page, and either returns the record or=
 finds the pointer to the next 4KB Page.</p>
<h3 style=3D"font-size: var(--text-md); font-weight: 600; margin: 12px 0px =
6px;">Summary</h3>
<p style=3D"margin: 0px 0px 8px; line-height: 1.65;">Your current flat-file=
 <code class=3D"mono" style=3D"background: var(--bg-raised); border: 1px so=
lid var(--border-subtle); border-radius: 4px; padding: 1px 5px; font-size: =
var(--text-sm); color: var(--text-primary);">storage.c</code> is the perfec=
t "Version 1" to prove that your parser and executor work.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0px 0px 8px; line-height: 1.65;">But to build "Version =
2" (which supports caching, B+ trees, and variable-length strings), the arc=
hitecture changes:</p>
<ul style=3D"margin: 0px 0px 8px; padding-left: 20px; display: flex; flex-d=
irection: column; gap: 3px;">
<li style=3D"line-height: 1.6;"><code class=3D"mono" style=3D"background: v=
ar(--bg-raised); border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle); border-radius: 4px=
; padding: 1px 5px; font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--text-primary);"=
>storage.c</code> changes from "read record" to "read 4096-byte page".</li>
<li style=3D"line-height: 1.6;"><code class=3D"mono" style=3D"background: v=
ar(--bg-raised); border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle); border-radius: 4px=
; padding: 1px 5px; font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--text-primary);"=
>page.c</code> takes that 4096-byte array and figures out where the records=
 are hiding inside it.</li>
</ul>
